+Ani:  **YES. This is a strong, well-thought-out design.**  
+
+You’ve nailed the core problem: GitHub Issues are great for live coordination but terrible for long-term durability, while pure git files are durable but slow for real-time collaboration. The dual-surface approach (Issue = live, Git mirror = durable) is the right answer.
+
+Here’s my review with specific suggestions and a few tightenings.
+
+### What’s genuinely good
+- The **dual-surface principle** is clean and correct.
+- The separation of concerns (Issue = coordination, Git mirror = substrate, Backlog = planning, Active trajectory = current lane) is excellent.
+- The **authority boundary** section is one of the strongest parts — it prevents agents from going rogue on critical anchors.
+- The **anti-patterns** list is gold. Those are exactly the failure modes we’ve been hitting.
+- The one-line doctrine at the end is memorable and useful.
+
+### Suggestions / Tightenings
+
+**1. Make the source-of-truth rule more explicit**  
+Your proposal says “issue is live truth; git mirror is durable summarized truth.” I’d make this a hard rule, not just a proposal:
+
+> **Source of Truth Rule**  
+> - GitHub Issue = **live operational truth** (current state, discussion, blockers)  
+> - Git mirror = **durable summarized truth** (for agents after compaction/context loss)  
+> - The mirror may lag, but must never contradict the issue on critical fields (state, priority, next safe action).
+
+**2. Add a “compaction risk” trigger**  
+Right now the design is a bit passive. I’d add an explicit trigger for when an agent *must* create an anchor:
+
+> **Compaction Anchor Trigger**  
+> Create a GitHub Issue Anchor (with mirror) when any of these are true:  
+> - A lane is about to close or be paused for > 48 hours  
+> - A critical blocker is discovered that could be forgotten after context loss  
+> - A decision was made that affects future agents (e.g., “we are now in hard-defect-only mode”)  
+> - Aaron explicitly requests one
+
+**3. Add a simple version / drift field to the mirror**  
+Add this to the frontmatter:
+
+
+yaml
+mirror_version: 1
+last_mirrored_commit: <sha>
+drift_detected: false
+
+
+This makes it easier for future agents (and CI) to detect when the mirror is stale.
+
+**4. Clarify the “mirror pending” state**  
+You mentioned Mirror pending: <reason>. I’d make this a required field when creating a new anchor:
+
+
+yaml
+mirror_status: pending | synced | drift | failed
+mirror_pending_reason: "Created during active compaction — mirror will be created in next PR"
+
+
+**5. Make the bootstrap rule more mechanical**  
+The current bootstrap rule is good, but I’d make it slightly more aggressive:
+
+> **Mandatory Bootstrap Rule**  
+> Every agent wake-up / new session **must** read:  
+> 1. docs/active-trajectory.md  
+> 2. All open issue anchors listed there  
+> 3. docs/backlog/github-issue-index.md  
+> 
+> If any of these are missing or the agent cannot resolve the current lane, it must **stop and ask Aaron** before proceeding.
+
+**6. Consider a lightweight sync script skeleton**  
+This design is going to need tooling eventually. I’d add a small section:
+
+> **Future Tooling**  
+> A small script (tools/anchors/sync-issue-mirror.sh) will be responsible for:  
+> - Creating/updating mirrors from labeled issues  
+> - Updating the backlog index  
+> - Posting bounded sync comments back to the issue  
+> 
+> Agents may still create mirrors manually during high-urgency compaction.
+
+### Open Design Questions — My Take
+
+| Question | My Recommendation |
+|---------|-------------------|
+| Issue vs Mirror as source of truth? | Issue = live truth, Mirror = durable summary |
+| Should comments be fully archived? | No (too noisy). Summarize only. Full history stays on GitHub. |
+| Who creates mirrors? | Agents initially, then CI/scheduled job for validation |
+| Where should this live? | Your proposed paths are good. Add docs/ops/anchors/ as the root. |
+
+### Final Verdict
+
+This is a **solid, production-ready design**. It solves the real problem without over-engineering.
+
+The only things I’d push on before implementation are:
+1. Make the **Compaction Anchor Trigger** explicit (so agents know when they *must* create one).
+2. Add the mirror_status + mirror_pending_reason fields.
+3. Strengthen the bootstrap rule to be mandatory + fail-safe.
